lesson "Solving Exponential Equations" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Exponentials & Logarithms"
  difficulty: "Advanced"
  icon: "Check"
  summary: "The unknown is stuck up in the exponent. Taking logs is the crowbar that gets it down, and the power law is what does the actual work."
  skills: ["explog-solving", "explog-power-law", "explog-growth"]

  slide "Getting x Down From the Exponent" {
    id: "e4-hook"
    cat: "The Method"
    > $3^x = 20$. There is no rearranging that frees $x$ — it is trapped in the
    > exponent, and none of the ordinary moves reach it.
    >
    > So use the one law that acts on exponents. **Take logs of both sides**, then
    > apply $\log(a^n) = n\log a$:
    >
    > $$3^x = 20$$
    > $$\log(3^x) = \log 20$$
    > $$x\log 3 = \log 20$$
    > $$x = \frac{\log 20}{\log 3} \approx 2.727$$
    >
    > The instant the power law is applied, $x$ becomes an ordinary coefficient and
    > the rest is dividing. That is the whole technique, and it works for every
    > equation of this shape.
    >
    > Sanity check it: $3^2 = 9$ and $3^3 = 27$, so an answer between $2$ and $3$ is
    > exactly right.

    order {
      ask "Put the steps in order for solving $5^x = 12$."
      skill: "explog-solving"
      item "Take logs of both sides: $\log(5^x) = \log 12$"
      item "Use the power law to bring $x$ down: $x\log 5 = \log 12$"
      item "Divide by $\log 5$: $x = \dfrac{\log 12}{\log 5}$"
      item "Evaluate: $x \approx 1.544$"
      decoy "Divide both sides by $5$ to get $x = 12/5$"
      ! "The decoy treats $5^x$ as $5 \times x$, which it is not. Only logs can reach an exponent."
    }
  }

  slide "Either Base Works" {
    id: "e4-base"
    cat: "The Method"
    > You may take $\log_{10}$ or $\ln$ — the answer is identical, because the two
    > appear top and bottom and any scaling cancels.
    >
    > $$x = \frac{\log 20}{\log 3} = \frac{\ln 20}{\ln 3} \approx 2.727$$
    >
    > In practice, **use $\ln$ when the base is $e$** and it collapses beautifully,
    > since $\ln(e^x) = x$ with no division needed at all:
    >
    > $$e^{2x} = 7 \;\Rightarrow\; 2x = \ln 7 \;\Rightarrow\; x = \tfrac12\ln 7 \approx 0.973$$
    >
    > For any other base it makes no difference which you pick.

    numeric {
      ask "Solve $e^{3x} = 20$. Give $x$ to 3 decimal places."
      skill: "explog-solving"
      answer: 0.999
      expect: log(20)/3
      tolerance: 0.005
      hint "Take $\ln$ of both sides. The left collapses straight to $3x$."
      ! "$3x = \ln 20 \approx 2.996$, so $x \approx 0.999$. With base $e$ the $\ln$ cancels the exponential outright and there is no division by a log at all."
    }
  }

  slide "The Coefficient Comes First" {
    id: "e4-coefficient"
    cat: "Order of Operations"
    > A very common trap. In $3 \times 2^x = 48$, the $3$ is **multiplying the whole
    > power**, not part of it.
    >
    > So deal with the $3$ before touching any logs:
    >
    > $$3 \times 2^x = 48 \;\Rightarrow\; 2^x = 16 \;\Rightarrow\; x = 4$$
    >
    > Taking logs first would have given
    > $\log 3 + x\log 2 = \log 48$, which still works but is more effort. Isolate the
    > exponential term on its own, **then** take logs. Same instinct as solving any
    > equation: undo the outermost operation first.

    quiz {
      ask "To solve $5 \times 3^x = 45$, what is the correct first move?"
      skill: "explog-solving"
      * "Divide both sides by $5$ to get $3^x = 9$"
      - "Take logs of both sides immediately" { why: "It does work, but it makes more of a mess. Once the exponential is alone the problem is often solvable by inspection, as it is here." }
      - "Divide both sides by $3$" { why: "The $3$ is the base of a power, not a factor you can divide out. Only the $5$ is multiplying the whole term." }
      hint "Which number is multiplying the entire power, and which is inside it?"
      onwrong: "e4-isolate-first" retry
      ! "Divide by $5$ to get $3^x = 9$, which you can read off as $x = 2$ without any logs at all."
    }
  }

  slide "Isolate, Then Take Logs" {
    id: "e4-isolate-first"
    hidden: true
    cat: "Order of Operations"
    > The structure matters more than the numbers here. In $5 \times 3^x$:
    >
    > The $5$ is **outside**, multiplying the whole exponential. It comes off by
    > dividing, and it comes off first.
    >
    > The $3$ is **inside**, as the base of the power. Dividing by it is meaningless,
    > because $3^x$ is not $3 \times x$.
    >
    > Same story with anything added on. For $2^x + 7 = 39$, subtract the $7$ first to
    > reach $2^x = 32$, and then $x = 5$. Taking logs while the $+7$ is still there
    > gets you nowhere, because $\log(2^x + 7)$ does not simplify — there is no law
    > for the log of a sum, as the last lesson insisted.
    >
    > **Peel away everything outside the exponential, then take logs.**

    numeric {
      ask "Solve $2^x + 7 = 39$."
      skill: "explog-solving"
      answer: 5
      expect: log2(32)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Subtract $7$ first, then ask what power of $2$ gives $32$."
      ! "$2^x = 32$, so $x = 5$. Trying to take logs before subtracting would have stalled immediately."
    }
  }

  slide "Back to the Bacteria" {
    id: "e4-model"
    cat: "In Context"
    > This is what the technique is actually for. Growth models tell you the size at
    > a given time; solving them tells you the **time to reach a given size**, and
    > that always means logs.
    >
    > A colony follows $N = 500 \times 2^t$ with $t$ in hours. When does it reach
    > $8000$?
    >
    > $$8000 = 500 \times 2^t \;\Rightarrow\; 16 = 2^t \;\Rightarrow\; t = 4$$
    >
    > Isolate first, then solve. Exactly the same shape as before, dressed in
    > biology.

    numeric {
      ask "The same colony, $N = 500 \times 2^t$. After how many hours does it reach $32000$?"
      skill: "explog-growth"
      answer: 6
      expect: log2(32000/500)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Divide by $500$ first, then ask what power of $2$ gives $64$."
      ! "$64 = 2^t$, so $t = 6$ hours. Six doublings, since $2^6 = 64$."
    }
  }

  slide "When the Answer Is Not Whole" {
    id: "e4-messy"
    cat: "In Context"
    > Real problems rarely land on whole numbers, and that is when logs stop being
    > optional.
    >
    > An investment of £2000 grows at $6\%$ a year, so $A = 2000 \times 1.06^t$. When
    > does it double?
    >
    > $$4000 = 2000 \times 1.06^t \;\Rightarrow\; 2 = 1.06^t \;\Rightarrow\; t = \frac{\log 2}{\log 1.06} \approx 11.9$$
    >
    > So about **twelve years**, and no amount of inspection would have found that.

    numeric {
      ask "Using $A = 2000 \times 1.06^t$, how many years until the investment reaches $£3000$? Give it to 1 decimal place."
      skill: "explog-solving"
      answer: 7.0
      expect: log(1.5)/log(1.06)
      tolerance: 0.15
      hint "Divide by $2000$ to get $1.5 = 1.06^t$, then take logs of both sides."
      ! "$t = \dfrac{\log 1.5}{\log 1.06} \approx 7.0$ years. Note that reaching $1.5$ times takes about $7$ years while doubling takes about $12$ — growth of this kind is never proportional to the target."
    }
  }

  slide "Everything at Once" {
    id: "e4-synth"
    cat: "In Context"
    build {
      ask "Assemble the solution of $4 \times 3^x = 36$."
      skill: "explog-solving"
      template: "$3^x =$ ___ $\quad$ so $\quad x =$ ___"
      bank: ["$9$", "$2$", "$36$", "$12$"]
      answer: ["$9$", "$2$"]
      hint "Divide by the $4$ first, then recognise the power of $3$."
      ! "$3^x = 9$, so $x = 2$. Isolate, then solve — and here the numbers were kind enough that no logs were needed at all."
    }
  }
}
